# The descent, played by bots. This is the same five floors the host plays in
# reference/host/Run.cs, with the choices taken out: no map, no rewards offered, no shop. What a
# scenario can say about a run is what happens if you take the middle of every decision, so the
# rewards are written in as the statements a bot cannot choose, and the shrine is a `heal`.
#
# The two lines at the bottom are what this is for. `wins`, `hp_left` and `turns` are printed and
# not checked: three members make every bot's hp heuristic worse rather than better, and under
# `turns: initiative` a bot that spends the Ferryman's step on a Punt has spent a whole step on
# a card that deals nothing. A win rate here is a fact about the bot.
#
# Run:  dotnet run --project src/Cantrip.Cli -- sim reference/content

scenario "The Drowned Chapel"
  runs 200

  player hp 40 max_hp 40 energy 3 speed 6
  hero Warden
  hero Cantor
  deck 3 Censer, 3 Litany, 2 Wade, Pike, Grace

  battle "Drowned Acolyte"

  relic "Drowned Coin"
  battle "Drowned Acolyte", "Choir of Teeth"

  # The shrine. The host offers a rest, a recruit or a raise here; a scenario takes one.
  heal 12
  hero Ferryman
  battle "Bell Warden", Tidewalker

  # The shop, spent for the party rather than chosen.
  relic "Choirmaster's Baton"
  deck Requiem, Aspersion
  battle "Bell Warden", "Drowned Acolyte", "Choir of Teeth"

  relic Tideglass
  battle "The Antiphonary"

  expect no stalls
  expect no errors
